# BURNDOWN: once commands are intuitive I should remove them from this cheatsheet
# This will keep the cheatsheet succinct for easier reference of the truly hard to remember commands.

# Movement

W               next start of whitespace-delimited word
E               next end of whitespace-delimited word
B               previous start of whitespace-delimited word

# Insertion

R               enter insert mode but replace instead of inserting chars
:r {file}       insert from file


# Search and replace with the `:substitute` (aka `:s`) command

:%s/foo/bar/gc      ask to manually confirm (`c` flag) each replacement
:'<,'>s/foo/bar/g   replace all matches in visual selection
:5,10s/foo/bar/g    replace all matches within line numbers

# Preceding a motion or edition with a number repeats it 'n' times
42G         go to line 42

# Shell manipulations
:.! <command>                 - Shell out
:'<,'>!jq -c                  - Format visual selected json
:'<,'>!sort -gk 1 -t ,        - To sort  a visual range on column 1 as a number

# Delete every line that has a FOO in it. See `:help global`.
# The _ in the d _ command ensures registers and clipboards are not changed.
:g/FOO/d _


# Macros
qa         " start recording into register 'a'
...        " do all your edits (navigate into block, add line, move to next block)
q          " stop recording
@a         " replay once
@@         " replay again
10@a       " replay 10 times


# Buffers
# move to N, next, previous, first last buffers
:ls              - show current buffers
:bn              - goes to next buffer
:bp              - goes to prev buffer
:bf              - goes to first buffer
:bl              - goes to last buffer
:b 2             - open buffer #2 in this window
:new             - open a new buffer
:vnew            - open a new vertical buffer
:bd 2            - deletes buffer 2
:wall            - writes all buffers
:ball            - open a window for all buffers
:bunload         - removes buffer from window
:taball          - open a tab for all buffers


# Commands summary

# Operators
=               auto-indent

# Motions
0 ^ $           line start / first non-blank / end
{ }             paragraph backward / forward
f t             find / till char (on line)
n N             next / prev search match


# Text objects (used after a modifier)
w               word
s               sentence
p               paragraph
b / (           parentheses
B / {           curly braces
[               square brackets
t               HTML/XML tag
" ' `           quoted string

